{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-102793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{-2}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}+2t{y}^{′}-2y=0$ for $t>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(t){y}_{1}(t)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(t)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{t}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{t}^{-2}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{-2}-2u{t}^{-3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{-2}-4{u}^{′}{t}^{-3}+6u{t}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{-2}-4{u}^{′}{t}^{-3}+6u{t}^{-4})+2t({u}^{′}{t}^{-2}-2u{t}^{-3})-2u{t}^{-2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}{t}^{-1}+6u{t}^{-2}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{-1}-4u{t}^{-2}-2u{t}^{-2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{-1}({u}^{″}t-2{u}^{′}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}({u}^{″}t-2{u}^{′}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{t}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nt{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51af07cbd1eec904ac5c54f9efbb45b2aab327adfa8de72c66bed543937ce6fb"} {"question" "q-102793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "t{u}^{″}-{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{t}^{2}{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}-2t{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}-{t}^{2}{u}^{′}=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-90510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then what equation must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-90510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-90510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51af07cbd1eec904ac5c54f9efbb45b2aab327adfa8de72c66bed543937ce6fb"} {"question" "q-90510" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-90510" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0"] ["math" "2{u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0"] ["math" "2{u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-90510" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-3t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-3y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(t){y}_{1}(t)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(t)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{e}^{-3t}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-3t}-3u{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-3t}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-6t}+9u{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-3t}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-6t}+9u{e}^{-3t})+2({u}^{′}{e}^{-3t}-3u{e}^{-3t})-3u{e}^{-3t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3t}({u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}+9u+2{u}^{′}-6u-3u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3t}({u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51af07cbd1eec904ac5c54f9efbb45b2aab327adfa8de72c66bed543937ce6fb"} {"question" "q-102792" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102792" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+2u=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}-2u=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}-8{u}^{′}=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102792" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}-5y=0$ for $x>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=ux.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=ux$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}x+u \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′})+5x({u}^{′}x+u)-5ux & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+5{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+5ux-5ux & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}+7{u}^{′}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}x+7{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nx{u}^{″}+7{u}^{′}=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51af07cbd1eec904ac5c54f9efbb45b2aab327adfa8de72c66bed543937ce6fb"} {"question" "q-64995" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64995" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "x{u}^{″}+3{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "x{u}^{″}+7{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "x{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "x{u}^{″}-5{u}^{′}=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64995" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x{u}^{″}+7{u}^{′}=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102794" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2x(x+1){y}^{′}+2(x+1)y=0$ for $x>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102794" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=ux.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=ux$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}x+u \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′})-2x(x+1)({u}^{′}x+u)+2(x+1)(ux) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}{x}^{2}(x+1)-2ux(x+1)+2ux(x+1) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}{x}^{3}-2{u}^{′}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}-2{u}^{′}{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102794" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51af07cbd1eec904ac5c54f9efbb45b2aab327adfa8de72c66bed543937ce6fb"} {"question" "q-102794" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102794" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{u}^{″}-2(x+1){u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}-4(x+1){u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{x}^{2}{u}^{″}-2(x+1){u}^{′}=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102794" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{4x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{4x}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+16u{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{4x}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+16u{e}^{4x})-3({u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x})-4u{e}^{4x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{4x}({u}^{″}+8{u}^{′}+16u-3{u}^{′}-12u-4u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{4x}({u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4x}\\ne 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=-5w.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-5x}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5}{e}^{-5x}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{4x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{4x}(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5}{e}^{-5x}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-5$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-5x}={e}^{-x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51af07cbd1eec904ac5c54f9efbb45b2aab327adfa8de72c66bed543937ce6fb"} {"question" "q-102800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "x{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{e}^{9x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-102800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}-3y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})-3u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-{u}^{′}x-3u-3u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-4$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51af07cbd1eec904ac5c54f9efbb45b2aab327adfa8de72c66bed543937ce6fb"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{2}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u)-5x({u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux)+8u{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u-5{u}^{′}x-10u+8u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}-{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x-w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51af07cbd1eec904ac5c54f9efbb45b2aab327adfa8de72c66bed543937ce6fb"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}={e}^{x}({u}^{′}+u) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={e}^{x}({u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+u)-2{e}^{x}({u}^{′}+u)+{e}^{x}(u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+u-2{u}^{′}-2u+u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}{u}^{″} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0\\,⟹\\,w={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot x=x{e}^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51af07cbd1eec904ac5c54f9efbb45b2aab327adfa8de72c66bed543937ce6fb"} {"question" "q-64953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=-6w\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{csc}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{csc}^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51af07cbd1eec904ac5c54f9efbb45b2aab327adfa8de72c66bed543937ce6fb"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "db15481a6dafed5c03e5b908e70d410230cb2eccbad0157a903c7eeb486ee675"} {"question" "e-6574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y_1(x) = e^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 6y' + 9y = 0.$ If $y_{2} = u(x) y_1(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$\n\n- $u''=0$\n\n- $e^{3x}u'' + u=0$\n\n- $u''-u=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/example-6574.json" "file_sha256" "eca9c9e60be6c7ad4182da3210911b57effcb3382d3aa079eaa492a02835a915"} {"question" "e-6574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =u(x){y}_{1}(x) \\\\  & =u(x){e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x})-6({u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x})+9u{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}({u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}+9u-6{u}^{′}-18u+9u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}{u}^{″} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{3x} \\neq 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/example-6574.json" "file_sha256" "eca9c9e60be6c7ad4182da3210911b57effcb3382d3aa079eaa492a02835a915"} {"question" "e-6573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y_1(t) = e^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 3y ' - 4y = 0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(t)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/example-6573.json" "file_sha256" "6ae15ea9c8259dd0d16436bc0be70f6975a3cbbd1dc49866288363f78d7ea07e"} {"question" "e-6573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2} = u(t)e^{-t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-t}-u{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{-t}+u{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{-t}+u{e}^{-t})-3({u}^{′}{e}^{-t}-u{e}^{-t})-4u{e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-5{u}^{′}{e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-t}({u}^{″}-5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^t \\neq 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' - 5u' = 0.\n$$\n\nLet $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation,\n\n$$\nw' - 5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = c_1 e^{5t}.\n$$\n\nNow, since since $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = c_1e^{5t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(t) = u e^{-t}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t}\\left( \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe can choose any values for $c_1$ and $c_2$ to yield a second solution. For example, if we set $c_1 = 5$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t} \\cdot e^{5t} = e^{4t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/example-6573.json" "file_sha256" "6ae15ea9c8259dd0d16436bc0be70f6975a3cbbd1dc49866288363f78d7ea07e"}] "usage" {"q-64958" [] "q-64995" [] "e-6573" [] "e-6574" []} "basis" 1986 "retractions" [[17592186125100 :question/problem "The function $y_{1}(x) = x$ is a solution to the equation $x^{2}y^{″} + 5xy^{′} - 5y = 0$ for $x > 0$. If $y_{2} = u(x)y_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)$?"] [17592186125098 :question/problem "The function $y_{1}(x) = x^{3}$ is a solution to the equation $x^{2}y^{″} - xy^{′} - 3y = 0$ for $x > 0$. Use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_{2}(x)$ to the equation."] [17592186124287 :question/problem "The function $y_1(x) = e^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 6y' + 9y = 0$. If $y_{2} = u(x) y_1(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)$?\n\n1. $u''=0$\n2. $e^{3x}u'' + u=0$\n3. $u''-u=0$"] [17592186124287 :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2}(x) &= u(x)y_{1}(x) \\\\\n&= u(x)e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2} &= ue^{3x} \\\\\ny_{2}^{′} &= u^{′}e^{3x} + 3ue^{3x} \\\\\ny_{2}^{″} &= u^{″}e^{3x} + 6u^{′}e^{3x} + 9ue^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″} - 6y^{′} + 9y &= 0 \\\\\n(u^{″}e^{3x} + 6u^{′}e^{3x} + 9ue^{3x}) - 6(u^{′}e^{3x} + 3ue^{3x}) + 9ue^{3x} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{3x}(u^{″} + 6u^{′} + 9u - 6u^{′} - 18u + 9u) &= 0 \\\\\ne^{3x}u^{″} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\ne^{3x} \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [17592186124286 :question/problem "Given that $y_1(t) = e^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 3y ' - 4y = 0$, use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(t)$ to the equation."] [17592186124286 :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let\n\n$$\ny_{2} = u(t)e^{-t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2} &= ue^{-t} \\\\\ny_{2}^{′} &= u^{′}e^{-t} - ue^{-t} \\\\\ny_{2}^{″} &= u^{″}e^{-t} - 2u^{′}e^{-t} + ue^{-t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″} - 3y^{′} - 4y &= 0 \\\\\n(u^{″}e^{-t} - 2u^{′}e^{-t} + ue^{-t}) - 3(u^{′}e^{-t} - ue^{-t}) - 4ue^{-t} &= 0 \\\\\nu^{″}e^{-t} - 5u^{′}e^{-t} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{-t}(u^{″} - 5u^{′}) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\ne^t \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\nu'' - 5u' = 0\n$$\n\nLet $w = u'$. Then our equation becomes a first-order equation,\n\n$$\nw' - 5w=0\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = c_1 e^{5t}\n$$\n\nNow, since since $w = u'$, we have\n\n$$\nu' = c_1e^{5t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = u e^{-t}\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t}\\left(\\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2 \\right)\n$$\n\nWe can choose any values for $c_1$ and $c_2$ to yield a second solution. For example, if we set $c_1 = 5$ and $c_2 = 0$, we get\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t} \\cdot e^{5t} = e^{4t}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186123611 17592186123612 17592186123613 17592186123614 17592186123615 17592186123623 17592186123624 17592186123625 17592186123626 17592186123627] "immutable_fields" [[17592186123610 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123610 :answer-field/id] [17592186123610 :answer-field/key] [17592186123610 :answer-field/type] [17592186123622 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123622 :answer-field/id] [17592186123622 :answer-field/key] [17592186123622 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
